#: src/Admin.php:146
msgid "When PostHog may record a visitor. Consent rules differ by country and by what you record, so this is your decision as the shop owner: 99Trace supports each choice and does not decide for you. The default records every visitor, which gives PostHog complete data; the other choices follow the answer visitors give your cookie banner (Cookiebot, or a plugin using the WP Consent API) for \"statistics\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:153
msgid "Track every visitor: I handle consent myself"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:154
msgid "Automatic: follow the cookie banner (Cookiebot plugin, else WP Consent API; with neither, PostHog stays off in the browser)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:155
msgid "Cookiebot: wait for \"statistics\" consent (also when Cookiebot is loaded by the theme or Google Tag Manager)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:156
msgid "WP Consent API: wait for \"statistics\" consent"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:157
msgid "My site calls posthog.opt_in_capturing() itself"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:159
msgid "<strong>Track every visitor</strong> (default) records everyone from the first page, never waits for a banner, and is never blocked by Cookiebot: PostHog and the shop store the visitor's ID in cookies and send events for every visitor. Choose it if you have decided that analytics needs no consent on this shop, or that you collect it another way. The other choices wait for \"statistics\" consent; what happens before the answer is set below. Cart and checkout events made before the answer are not sent."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:162
msgid "Before the visitor answers the banner"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:167
msgid "Wait: load PostHog only after consent (nothing before)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:168
msgid "Record the visit, send it when the visitor accepts"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:169
msgid "Load PostHog switched off: experiments only (nothing recorded)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:171
msgid "Only with Cookiebot or the WP Consent API. <strong>Wait</strong>: PostHog starts after consent; what the visitor did before is not recorded. <strong>Record the visit</strong>: PostHog records pages and clicks in the page's memory, stores nothing in the browser and sends nothing; when the visitor accepts, all of it is sent under one visitor and PostHog runs normally from then on; on a refusal the recording is thrown away. <strong>Switched off</strong>: experiments get their variant at once and nothing is recorded; on consent the page view and the experiment exposure are sent (needs Browser features → Feature flags and experiments; without it, this works as Wait). With Record the visit and Switched off, PostHog is asked for experiment variants before consent."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:173
msgid "Record the visit: keep it across pages until the answer"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:173
msgid "Off: only the page where the visitor answers is sent. On: the recording of earlier pages is kept in the browser's session storage (deleted when the tab closes) and sent too. This stores data in the browser before consent: switch it on only where that is allowed."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:175
msgid "Orders without consent"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:180
msgid "Counted anonymously (no visitor, IP or person)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:181
msgid "Not sent"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:183
msgid "An order whose buyer refused (or never answered) the banner. <strong>Counted anonymously</strong> (default): the purchase and its revenue are sent without anything that identifies the buyer, so revenue in PostHog stays complete. <strong>Not sent</strong>: PostHog does not see it. A buyer who accepts on the thank-you page before the order is sent (Wait before sending an order) gets the order linked to their visit."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:190
msgid "Every event 99Trace sends to PostHog. Switch an event off, or give it your own name in PostHog (leave the name empty for the standard one). A renamed event is still sent exactly once; reports, funnels and destinations in PostHog that use the old name must be changed to the new one. PostHog's own automatic events can be switched off but keep their names, because PostHog's built-in reports rely on them. Browser and PostHog events need Connect → Run PostHog in the browser."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:193
msgid "Carts the checkout page fills"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:198
msgid "Not sent (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:199
msgid "Sent as Product Added"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:201
msgid "Some checkout pages, such as FunnelKit funnels, put the product in the cart when they open. The shopper did not add it, and Checkout Started already records the visit with its products. <strong>Not sent</strong> (default): Product Added only when the shopper adds a product (a button, a link, a change on the cart or the checkout). <strong>Sent</strong>: every such page load also sends Product Added, marked <code>source: checkout_page</code>, and ad platforms fed from Product Added count it as an add to cart."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:210
msgid "PostHog's setting (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:212
#: src/Admin.php:390
msgid "Always on"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:214
#: src/Admin.php:391
msgid "Always off"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:221
msgid "PostHog's setting decides whether it is on; this shop can only switch it off."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:225
msgid "PostHog features in the visitor's browser. Most of them have a switch in the PostHog project's own settings (Settings → Project in PostHog). <strong>PostHog's setting</strong> (recommended) leaves the choice to that switch, so you change it in PostHog; <strong>Always on</strong> or <strong>Always off</strong> override it on this shop. A change in PostHog reaches pages opened after it, not a page that is already open. On order and account pages replay, heatmaps and element text are always off, because they show the buyer's name and address."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:228
msgid "Rage clicks"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:228
msgid "Several quick clicks on the same spot: a sign of frustration. PostHog has no switch for this one."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:229
msgid "Feature flags and experiments"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:229
msgid "Lets PostHog experiments and feature flags decide what a visitor sees. Off: no experiment can run on this shop."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:230
msgid "One visitor across subdomains"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:230
msgid "Recognise the same visitor on shop.example.com and www.example.com."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:231
msgid "Hide all text"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:231
msgid "Clicks and recordings never contain the text on the page, on every page (not only order and account pages)."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:238
msgid "Not recorded (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:239
msgid "Recorded"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:242
msgid "How visitors and orders appear in PostHog."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:244
msgid "Shop staff"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:249
msgid "Signed-in users who can manage orders: administrators and shop managers. <strong>Not recorded</strong> (default): nothing from them reaches PostHog, so no page views, recordings, experiments, carts or checkouts. A purchase they make is still counted, without their visits."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:252
msgid "Page editors"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:257
msgid "Signed-in users who can edit pages but not manage orders, such as editors and custom roles for building pages. Their test visits to product and checkout pages would otherwise count as shopping, also in ad platforms fed from PostHog. <strong>Not recorded</strong> (default) works as for shop staff."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:260
msgid "Preview loads"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:265
msgid "Pages opened in a page builder or as a preview: Elementor's editor and preview, FunnelKit's previews, WordPress page previews and the Customizer, with the calls they make while open. Only signed-in users can open them. <strong>Not recorded</strong> (default): never sent, whoever opens them."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:268
msgid "Person profiles"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:273
msgid "Every visitor (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:274
msgid "Only logged-in customers"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:276
msgid "A person profile is the card in PostHog (People) that collects everything one visitor did: pages and clicks from the browser, and purchases and refunds from the shop's server. <strong>Every visitor</strong> gives each visitor a card, so you can open a buyer and see their whole journey. <strong>Only logged-in customers</strong> creates cards only for customers who sign in; other visitors' events are still counted but belong to no card (PostHog bills these \"anonymous\" events at a lower rate). Browser and server events always follow the same choice."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:278
msgid "Recognise logged-in customers on every device"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:278
msgid "A customer who signs in is joined to one person in PostHog, on every device they use. The account is named by an anonymous number, never by e-mail address or name."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:281
msgid "One visitor ID for the browser and the shop"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:282
msgid "Cart, coupon, checkout and order events are sent by the shop's server; page views and clicks by PostHog in the browser. With this on, both use one visitor ID, kept in a first-party cookie (<code>ph_wc_vid</code>) that the server can read: whichever side needs an ID first creates it, and the other uses the same one. So an \"add to cart\" clicked before PostHog has loaded, or by a visitor whose ad blocker stops PostHog, lands on the same person as the rest of the visit and the order, without PostHog having to merge two visitors. The cookie is written only where the visitor may be recorded (Consent). Off: cart and checkout events are sent only when PostHog's own cookie is there."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:284
msgid "Customer details on the person"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:284
msgid "Sets the buyer's e-mail, first and last name and phone as properties of their person in PostHog: as soon as they are typed on the checkout, when an order is paid, and when a customer signs in or creates an account. Only for visitors who may be recorded (Consent), never on an anonymous order, and never inside events or web addresses. PostHog receives them as typed; PostHog's destinations (Meta, Google Ads) hash them just before sending. Off: none of these details reach PostHog."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:285
msgid "Join the thank-you page's browser to the buyer"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:285
msgid "Buyers who pay in a bank app often come back in another browser (for example from Instagram to Safari). With this on, that browser continues as the same visitor, so the visit and the purchase stay on one person. Also: an order placed before the visitor answered the cookie banner is linked to them when they accept on its thank-you page, as long as it has not been sent yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:286
msgid "Locate buyers by their own IP address"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:286
msgid "Purchases are sent by the shop's server; this passes the buyer's IP address so PostHog shows the buyer's country, not the server's."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:288
msgid "Wait before sending an order (minutes)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:296
msgid "A one-click upsell after payment is added to the order: waiting lets it be part of the purchase PostHog receives. The purchase keeps the time of payment. 0–120; 10 recommended."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:303
msgid "For developers and special cases."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:305
msgid "URL parameters never sent"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin.php:309
msgid "Comma-separated. These parameters are removed from every web address PostHog receives, at any depth. Order keys (wc_order_…) and e-mail addresses are always removed."
msgstr ""

